tree with your prediction.

213
00:16:39,227 --> 00:16:43,497
Q63: "Why are --assume-unchanged and
--skip-worktree not ways to ignore local

214
00:16:43,537 --> 00:16:47,752
changes? What do you recommend to a team
that needs per-developer settings?"

215
00:16:50,092 --> 00:16:55,045
Answer out loud. A strong answer states
what each bit is documented for, describes

216
00:16:55,085 --> 00:17:00,100
how each one fails when the file changes
upstream, and quotes Git's own position on

217
00:17:00,140 --> 00:17:05,155
the underlying wish. The recommendation
should be concrete enough to implement, and

218
00:17:05,195 --> 00:17:08,017
should say why the private
file's name matters.

219
00:17:09,857 --> 00:17:15,557
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say, in your own words: git ls-files

220
00:17:15,597 --> 00:17:21,587
lists index entries and, with options,
untracked and ignored paths, and scripts use

221
00:17:21,627 --> 00:17:27,545
it with --exclude-standard and -z. The
assume-unchanged and skip-worktree bits are

222
00:17:27,585 --> 00:17:33,285
promises to Git, not an ignore mechanism.
Private settings belong in a separate

223
00:17:33,325 --> 00:17:34,230
ignored file.

224
00:17:34,570 --> 00:17:39,871
During a conflict a path has entries at
stages 1, 2 and 3: base, ours, theirs.

225
00:17:40,211 --> 00:17:47,355
The index caches stat data, so plumbing
can report an unchanged file as modified

226
00:17:47,395 --> 00:17:53,103
until the index is refreshed. A
stale index.lock blocks writers.

227
00:17:53,143 --> 00:17:59,929
A corrupt index is rebuilt from HEAD
with git reset, and what is lost is the

228
00:17:59,969 --> 00:18:00,647
staging.

